      {
        "id": 97594,
        "name": "Azqa",
        "username": "Azqa",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/azqa/{size}/2015_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-05-29T04:23:35.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"jffdougan\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/jffdougan/48/1882_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> jffdougan:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>See, I'm strange on this front – my favorites are still absolutely Summer Knight and Small Favor. What I enjoyed most about this one was that it feels more like one of the pre-Changes books.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I agree that it felt more like the earlier casefiles than the mini-trilogy of Changes, Ghost Story, and Cold Days, and I hope he continues in that vein. &nbsp;I understand why Butcher had to tear down (in Changes) so much of what he'd had Harry build up, but it was also frustrating to see that character not just plateau but actually regress in many areas. &nbsp;Skin Game did a great job of pulling together Harry's support network, his mixture of preparation and improvisation on a personal level, and the foresight he'd finally started showing back around Turn Coat. &nbsp;I'd missed those in the past couple books, even if there had to be some time for Harry to really break into the big leagues.</p>",
        "post_number": 77,
        "post_type": 1,
